import{_ as t,o,c as a,a3 as s}from"./chunks/framework.DOLRlNb_.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Releasing a beta","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/releasing.md","filePath":"docs/releasing.md"}'),d={name:"docs/releasing.md"};function i(n,e,r,h,l,c){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="releasing-a-beta" tabindex="-1">Releasing a beta <a class="header-anchor" href="#releasing-a-beta" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Releasing a beta&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Coach Poulpy is in beta. <strong>Pushing a version tag is the release:</strong> the tag is the version, CI runs in full, then a GitHub pre-release with the Android APK and the server image on the GitHub Container Registry are published. No version pull request, no button, no agent. Deploying the server is a separate button. A release plus the deployed server is production; the app stores come in their own phase (<a href="./roadmap">roadmap</a>).</p><h2 id="the-version" tabindex="-1">The version <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-version" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The version&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The version people see (Settings, &quot;Version 0.6.0&quot;) is the tag without its <code>v</code>. The Release workflow passes it to the builds as <code>POULPY_VERSION_NAME</code>: Android uses it as <code>versionName</code> and iOS as <code>MARKETING_VERSION</code>. Every other build, on a computer or in CI, says &quot;Version dev&quot;. A tag is three numbers after a <code>v</code>, like <code>v0.6.0</code>; while in beta it stays <code>v0.x.y</code>, and a release is a pre-release until the first <code>v1.0.0</code>.</p><p>The Android <code>versionCode</code> is the minute the build ran (minutes since 2026-01-01), as in CI, so a release installs over any earlier build.</p><h2 id="cut-a-release" tabindex="-1">Cut a release <a class="header-anchor" href="#cut-a-release" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cut a release&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p>Make sure <code>main</code> is where you want it and CI is green on it.</p></li><li><p>Tag it and push the tag:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> switch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> main</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> &amp;&amp; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> pull</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> tag</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> v0.6.0</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> push</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> origin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> v0.6.0</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p><strong>Actions &gt; Release</strong> starts on its own. It runs the whole of CI (shared tests, server, Android APK, iOS build, website), then publishes.</p></li><li><p>Once it is done, the <strong>Releases</strong> page has <code>v0.6.0</code> with <code>coach-poulpy-v0.6.0.apk</code>, <code>coach-poulpy-deploy.tar.gz</code> and notes listing the merged pull requests since the last one.</p></li><li><p>On a phone, download the APK from that page and install it over the old one; the phone&#39;s data is kept.</p></li></ol><p>Creating the release from GitHub&#39;s <strong>Releases</strong> page also works, but it is visible before CI passes and stays without an APK if CI fails. Push the tag instead.</p><p>The workflow stops with a message, and publishes nothing, if the tag is not <code>v</code> and three numbers, if the signing key secrets are missing (see <a href="#the-signing-key">The signing key</a>), or if any CI check fails. To try again, delete the tag (<code>git push origin :refs/tags/v0.6.0</code> and <code>git tag -d v0.6.0</code>), fix <code>main</code>, and tag again.</p><h2 id="the-server" tabindex="-1">The server <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-server" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The server&quot;">​</a></h2><p>After CI, the same workflow pushes two images with the version baked in: <code>ghcr.io/tkint/coach-poulpy-server</code> and <code>ghcr.io/tkint/coach-poulpy-web</code>, each tagged <code>0.2.0</code>, <code>0.2</code> and <code>latest</code> (amd64 only, <a href="./adr/0019-the-server-runs-with-docker-compose-on-one-server">ADR 0019</a>). <code>/health</code> then answers <code>ok 0.2.0</code>; any other build says <code>ok dev</code>. The pre-release is created only once both images are pushed, with <code>coach-poulpy-deploy.tar.gz</code> holding that version&#39;s Compose and Caddy files.</p><p>A package&#39;s first push makes it private. Make it public once in its <strong>Package settings</strong>, or log the machine in with <code>docker login ghcr.io</code>.</p><h2 id="deploy-the-server" tabindex="-1">Deploy the server <a class="header-anchor" href="#deploy-the-server" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Deploy the server&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A release never reaches the machine on its own. Open <strong>Actions &gt; Deploy &gt; Run workflow</strong>, pick what to deploy (<code>server</code>, <code>website</code> or <code>all</code>, server first), the <code>production</code> environment and <code>latest</code> or a version such as <code>0.2.0</code>. With <code>all</code>, a failed server deploy stops before the website. What happens on the machine is in <a href="./deploying-the-server#deploy">Deploying the server</a>.</p><p>Set it up once:</p><ol><li><p>On the machine, create a user in the <code>docker</code> group, and give it a key used only for deploys:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ssh-keygen</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -t</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ed25519</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -N</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -f</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> coach-poulpy-deploy</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -C</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> coach-poulpy-deploy</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># Add coach-poulpy-deploy.pub to that user&#39;s ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on the machine.</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p>On GitHub, <strong>Settings &gt; Environments</strong>, create <code>production</code> and add:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Kind</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>DEPLOY_SSH_KEY</code></td><td>secret</td><td>The private key file</td></tr><tr><td><code>DEPLOY_HOST</code></td><td>variable</td><td>The machine&#39;s address</td></tr><tr><td><code>DEPLOY_USER</code></td><td>variable</td><td>The user from step 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>DEPLOY_HOST_KEY</code></td><td>variable</td><td>The output of <code>ssh-keyscan -t ed25519 HOST</code>, so the workflow refuses any other machine</td></tr><tr><td><code>DEPLOY_PORT</code></td><td>variable</td><td>Optional, <code>22</code> by default</td></tr><tr><td><code>DOMAIN</code></td><td>variable</td><td>The API hostname, without scheme, port or path</td></tr><tr><td><code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code></td><td>secret</td><td>For example <code>openssl rand -hex 24</code>; used when the Postgres volume is created</td></tr><tr><td><code>SMTP_HOST</code></td><td>variable</td><td>The EU mail provider&#39;s SMTP server. Optional until one is chosen; see <a href="./deploying-the-server#mail">Mail</a> for what happens without it</td></tr><tr><td><code>SMTP_PORT</code></td><td>variable</td><td>Optional, <code>587</code> (STARTTLS) by default; <code>465</code> for implicit TLS</td></tr><tr><td><code>SMTP_USER</code></td><td>variable</td><td>The SMTP login</td></tr><tr><td><code>SMTP_PASSWORD</code></td><td>secret</td><td>The SMTP password</td></tr><tr><td><code>MAIL_FROM</code></td><td>variable</td><td>The sender, for example <code>Coach Poulpy &lt;hello@coach-poulpy.com&gt;</code>; needed with <code>SMTP_HOST</code>. <a href="./deploying-the-server#mail">Mail</a> says what a saved from-address changes</td></tr><tr><td><code>WEBSITE_URL</code></td><td>variable</td><td>Optional, <code>https://app.coach-poulpy.com</code> by default: where the links in emails point</td></tr><tr><td><code>ADMIN_EMAIL</code></td><td>variable</td><td>Optional: the confirmed account with this address becomes an admin when the server starts (<a href="./admin">the admin area</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LOKI_URL</code></td><td>variable</td><td>Optional: the push URL of a Grafana Cloud Loki, <code>https://logs-prod-XXX.grafana.net/loki/api/v1/push</code>. With it, the deploy starts a container that ships the logs there (<a href="./deploying-the-server#logs">Logs</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LOKI_USER</code></td><td>variable</td><td>The Loki user ID, a number; needed with <code>LOKI_URL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>LOKI_PASSWORD</code></td><td>secret</td><td>A Grafana Cloud access policy token that can only write logs; needed with <code>LOKI_URL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>WEB_DOMAIN</code></td><td>variable</td><td>The web app&#39;s hostname, such as <code>app.coach-poulpy.com</code>; needed only to deploy <code>website</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The mail settings (<code>SMTP_*</code>, <code>MAIL_FROM</code>) may be variables or secrets, in the environment or in the repository&#39;s <strong>Settings &gt; Secrets and variables &gt; Actions</strong>; a value in the environment wins.</p></li><li><p>In the same <code>production</code> environment, tick <strong>Required reviewers</strong> and add yourself, so a deploy waits for your approval before it touches the machine.</p></li></ol><p>The workflow stops with a message, and changes nothing, if a setting is missing or the version has characters other than letters, digits, <code>.</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>. Once it succeeds, <code>curl https://api.coach-poulpy.com/health</code> prints the version deployed. Deploying an older version is the way back if a release goes wrong.</p><h2 id="deploy-a-pull-request" tabindex="-1">Deploy a pull request <a class="header-anchor" href="#deploy-a-pull-request" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Deploy a pull request&quot;">​</a></h2><p>To try a pull request on the machine before it merges, open <strong>Actions &gt; Deploy &gt; Run workflow</strong>, keep <code>main</code> under <strong>Use workflow from</strong>, pick the environment and enter the pull request&#39;s number as the version, such as <code>275</code>. The workflow builds the pull request&#39;s latest commit, pushes it as <code>ghcr.io/tkint/coach-poulpy-server:pr-&lt;number&gt;</code> (or <code>coach-poulpy-web:pr-&lt;number&gt;</code> for <code>website</code>), and deploys it with the pull request&#39;s own Compose and Caddy files, since it may change them. <code>/health</code> then answers <code>ok pr-&lt;number&gt;</code>. Only open pull requests from branches of this repository are deployed, never forks. Nothing runs until you start it.</p><p>A branch with no pull request works too: pick it under <strong>Use workflow from</strong> and enter the version <code>branch</code>. Its image is <code>branch-&lt;branch name&gt;</code> (slashes become dashes).</p><p>Set up the <code>preview</code> environment once, like <code>production</code> above (the same <code>DEPLOY_*</code> settings). Its <code>APP_NAME</code> decides where the branch lands:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th><code>APP_NAME</code> in <code>preview</code></th><th><code>DOMAIN</code> in <code>preview</code></th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Another name, such as <code>coach-poulpy-preview</code></td><td>Its own hostname, such as <code>preview.api.coach-poulpy.com</code></td><td>A second server and Postgres in <code>~/coach-poulpy-preview</code>, beside production, with its own data</td></tr><tr><td>Unset, or <code>coach-poulpy</code></td><td>Production&#39;s</td><td>Replaces production until the next production deploy</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>APP_NAME</code> (lowercase letters, digits and dashes) names the folder, the Compose project and the Caddy route; production leaves it unset. Nothing is cleaned up on its own: deploy over it, or stop it with <code>cd ~/coach-poulpy-preview &amp;&amp; docker compose down</code>. A separate preview needs about as much memory as production.</p><p>Deploying runs the pull request&#39;s or branch&#39;s deploy files on the machine and replaces the shared <code>~/caddy/deploy.py</code> with its copy, so only deploy code you have read.</p><h2 id="installing-a-build-on-a-phone" tabindex="-1">Installing a build on a phone <a class="header-anchor" href="#installing-a-build-on-a-phone" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Installing a build on a phone&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Android:</strong> every CI run uploads a debug APK as the <code>coach-poulpy-debug-apk</code> artifact. Download it on the phone and allow installing from that source. Each build is numbered by the time it ran and signed with the same key, so a newer APK installs over the old one and keeps the phone&#39;s data. Android refuses an APK built before the one on the phone (&quot;you can&#39;t install the app on your device&quot;); to install an older branch&#39;s build, re-run its CI job, which gives it a fresh number. <code>./gradlew :app-android:assembleRelease</code> gives a smaller build, signed with the same key.</li><li><strong>iPhone:</strong> set your Apple team in Xcode (Signing &amp; Capabilities) and run on a connected iPhone. With a free Apple account the app expires after 7 days; TestFlight needs the paid Apple Developer Program.</li></ul><h2 id="the-signing-key" tabindex="-1">The signing key <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-signing-key" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The signing key&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Android only installs an update when it is signed with the same key as the app already on the phone. CI signs every APK with one fixed key, kept in two repository secrets:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Secret</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ANDROID_KEYSTORE_BASE64</code></td><td>The keystore file (<code>.jks</code>), base64 encoded on one line</td></tr><tr><td><code>ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD</code></td><td>Its password (the key inside, <code>poulpy</code>, uses the same one)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>To make them, run <code>tools/make-signing-key.sh</code> once on your own computer (it needs a JDK) and follow what it prints. Keep the <code>.jks</code> file and its password in a password manager: losing them means every phone has to uninstall the app once more. Without the secrets, for example on a pull request from a fork, CI still builds but signs with a throwaway key and says so in a warning.</p><p>To sign local builds with the same key, put <code>storeFile</code> and <code>storePassword</code> in <code>app-android/keystore.properties</code>, which is not committed. The build also reads <code>POULPY_KEYSTORE_FILE</code>, <code>POULPY_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD</code>, and optionally <code>POULPY_KEY_ALIAS</code> and <code>POULPY_KEY_PASSWORD</code>, from the environment.</p><h2 id="what-a-release-does-not-do-yet" tabindex="-1">What a release does not do yet <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-release-does-not-do-yet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What a release does not do yet&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>iPhone:</strong> no file is attached. A phone build needs an Apple Developer account and signing; until then iOS is built from Xcode (<a href="#installing-a-build-on-a-phone">Installing a build on a phone</a>).</li><li><strong>Debug build type:</strong> the APK is the same <code>assembleDebug</code> build CI makes, the one that has been run on phones. A smaller, minified <code>assembleRelease</code> build is a later step once it has been tried on a phone.</li><li><strong>Not a store release:</strong> while the major is 0, the pre-release flag keeps it off the &quot;latest&quot; release, and nothing goes to Google Play or TestFlight.</li></ul>`,33)])])}const g=t(d,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
